for the second paper in this mini workshop

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:16.910
Does Public Diplomacy Sway
Foreign Public Opinion?

00:00:16.910 --> 00:00:19.270
Identifying the Effect
of High Level Visits

00:00:19.270 --> 00:00:22.363
by Benjamin Goldsmith, Yusaku
Horiuchi and Kelly Matush.

00:00:23.230 --> 00:00:24.930
So I believe Yusaku is taking the lead

00:00:24.930 --> 00:00:29.173
on the presentation of this
paper, so please take it away.

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:34.860
- Okay, thank you very much
for giving us an opportunity

00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:36.885
to present our papers.

00:00:36.885 --> 00:00:39.960
So this is a paper which
then goes to me from ANU

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:42.400
and Kelly Matush from Florida State

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:45.150
and the title of this paper is

00:00:46.359 --> 00:00:50.053
does public diplomacy sway
foreign public opinion.

00:00:51.380 --> 00:00:56.270
So the many governments
invest significant resources

00:00:56.270 --> 00:00:58.810
to communicate with foreign citizens

00:00:58.810 --> 00:01:01.790
and this statement of government
sponsored communication

00:01:01.790 --> 00:01:03.150
often called public diplomacy

00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:05.460
represent a prominent component

00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:08.710
of state's foreign policy today.

00:01:08.710 --> 00:01:10.910
And the public diplomacy
activities include

00:01:10.910 --> 00:01:14.810
government sponsored
educational exchange programs,

00:01:14.810 --> 00:01:18.393
state broadcasting outlets,
and cultural events.

00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:23.130
And these efforts are aimed

00:01:23.130 --> 00:01:28.130
to shape global affairs through
improving the perception

00:01:28.676 --> 00:01:31.320
of their leaders, country, people

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:34.250
and core values and increasing support

00:01:34.250 --> 00:01:36.230
for specific policies.

00:01:36.230 --> 00:01:37.560
To put it differently,

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:39.350
these different medical activities

00:01:39.350 --> 00:01:43.010
are aimed to increase soft power resources

00:01:43.010 --> 00:01:45.673
which we have been discussing
since the first paper.

00:01:47.870 --> 00:01:50.890
So this the study of public
diplomacy has bloomed

00:01:50.890 --> 00:01:53.150
into a substantial literature

00:01:53.150 --> 00:01:57.780
but there's surprisingly
little well identified evidence

00:01:57.780 --> 00:02:01.243
about the effectiveness
of public diplomacy.

00:02:02.610 --> 00:02:05.610
Can government actually sway the opinion

00:02:05.610 --> 00:02:09.673
of foreign citizens with
such diplomatic efforts?

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:13.900
So we argued that this
fundamental question

00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:16.890
is essential to understand the impact

00:02:16.890 --> 00:02:20.400
of strategic transnational communication

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:22.813
in modern international relations.

00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:28.740
So to answer this question,
we emphasize the importance

00:02:28.740 --> 00:02:32.690
of expanding the scope of
public diplomacy studies,

00:02:32.690 --> 00:02:36.580
broad literature on
soft power, as you know

00:02:36.580 --> 00:02:40.500
is US-centric in its
(indistinct) exposition.

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:42.900
And empirical study this
also tends to be based

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:45.960
on things that countries
most often the United States,

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:47.843
or at most fit.

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:51.270
This focus on single country studies

00:02:51.270 --> 00:02:53.940
or maybe consult power does not allow us

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:57.550
to answer even basic ethical questions

00:02:57.550 --> 00:03:00.180
about the general use of public diplomacy.

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:02.850
For example, questions about Japan, China

00:03:02.850 --> 00:03:05.420
soft about competition, the
topic of the first paper

00:03:05.420 --> 00:03:06.760
at this conference

00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:10.770
or the impact of South Korean diplomacy.

00:03:12.350 --> 00:03:14.980
Those topics cannot be
well addressed empirically

00:03:14.980 --> 00:03:18.340
without a general
understanding of the efficacy

00:03:18.340 --> 00:03:21.530
of public diplomacy across countries.

00:03:21.530 --> 00:03:23.760
So to contribute to the literature

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:25.710
of public diplomacy and the soft power,

00:03:26.950 --> 00:03:30.200
we focused on the major
and ubiquitous tool

00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:33.110
of public diplomacy that is,

00:03:33.110 --> 00:03:36.703
high-level political leaders'
visits to other countries.

00:03:39.430 --> 00:03:43.610
So our study speaks to
several important issues

00:03:43.610 --> 00:03:45.150
in international relations

00:03:45.150 --> 00:03:48.230
beyond public diplomacy diplomacy.

00:03:48.230 --> 00:03:51.350
So transnational communication
is seen as increasing

00:03:51.350 --> 00:03:53.990
in relevance to international power

00:03:53.990 --> 00:03:56.830
in the global information age,

00:03:56.830 --> 00:03:58.300
and establishing that,

00:03:58.300 --> 00:04:01.160
leaders can shape public opinion abroad

00:04:01.160 --> 00:04:04.920
is a fundamental step to understanding how

00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:08.000
and when States achieve
their foreign policy goals

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:09.343
in this context.

00:04:10.780 --> 00:04:12.840
More broadly, public support

00:04:12.840 --> 00:04:16.090
is relevant to a wide range
of international goals.

00:04:16.090 --> 00:04:18.210
For example, existing studies

00:04:18.210 --> 00:04:20.630
suggest that public opinion contributes

00:04:20.630 --> 00:04:24.950
to a state's policy, states
policy on international trade,

00:04:24.950 --> 00:04:27.120
foreign aid, military coalitions

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:28.980
and (indistinct) basis.

00:04:28.980 --> 00:04:33.090
As a result, cultivating
for a public goodwill,

00:04:33.090 --> 00:04:37.650
may assist a state in shaping
apart from our state's ability

00:04:37.650 --> 00:04:40.080
to implement a design and a policy

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:42.973
across a wide range of policy regimes.

00:04:45.210 --> 00:04:48.860
So leaders around the world,
devote significant amounts

00:04:48.860 --> 00:04:51.470
of their limited time
to international travel.

00:04:51.470 --> 00:04:54.360
According to the recent
study by Maurice and Semis,

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:58.420
recent US presidents spent
up to one side of their time

00:04:58.420 --> 00:04:59.813
on international trips.

00:05:00.750 --> 00:05:04.160
The primary reason leaders
travel internationally

00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:08.300
is to have a closed door
negotiation with host leaders,

00:05:08.300 --> 00:05:10.930
a host country leaders to discuss security

00:05:10.930 --> 00:05:14.740
and economic issues or
other important issues.

00:05:14.740 --> 00:05:16.403
But during high-level visit,

00:05:16.403 --> 00:05:20.330
foreign leaders engage in
extensive public outreach

00:05:20.330 --> 00:05:23.210
deliberately addressing foreign audiences

00:05:23.210 --> 00:05:25.220
and attending public events

00:05:25.220 --> 00:05:26.853
in front of TV cameras.

00:05:28.103 --> 00:05:33.027
For example, visiting and a
host leaders may play charts.

00:05:34.250 --> 00:05:37.310
They may have Sushi together
as a famous restaurant

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:40.670
or they may enjoy playing golf together

00:05:41.950 --> 00:05:44.250
or the visiting leader maybe invited

00:05:44.250 --> 00:05:46.620
to attend a Sumo tournament.

00:05:46.620 --> 00:05:51.620
So why do they attend public
events in front of TV cameras.

00:05:52.070 --> 00:05:55.360
Given the intense
constraints on their time,

00:05:55.360 --> 00:05:59.150
the fact that visiting
often spend significant time

00:05:59.150 --> 00:06:02.210
on public outreach while abroad

00:06:02.210 --> 00:06:04.660
should suggest that they believe

00:06:04.660 --> 00:06:08.673
this type of public diplomacy
brings substantial benefits.

00:06:10.200 --> 00:06:14.360
We argue that they engage
in these public outreach

00:06:14.360 --> 00:06:16.440
and image building activities

00:06:16.440 --> 00:06:19.955
because a visiting
leaders wants to influence

00:06:19.955 --> 00:06:20.810
foreign public opinion.

00:06:20.810 --> 00:06:23.330
In other words, these events

00:06:23.330 --> 00:06:25.120
outside the closed door meetings

00:06:25.120 --> 00:06:28.850
are part of their public
diplomatic campaigns.

00:06:28.850 --> 00:06:30.500
There are two ways visiting leaders

00:06:30.500 --> 00:06:32.453
can sway foreign public opinion.

00:06:33.350 --> 00:06:37.140
First, a visiting leader
try to increase awareness

00:06:37.140 --> 00:06:39.560
of themselves and their country

00:06:39.560 --> 00:06:42.800
among citizens in the host country.

00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:46.300
Of many possible tools of public diplomacy

00:06:46.300 --> 00:06:48.690
high-level visits are
particularly well suited

00:06:48.690 --> 00:06:51.180
to gain the audience attention

00:06:51.180 --> 00:06:54.893
due to the large amount of media
coverage they tend to draw.

00:06:55.890 --> 00:07:00.120
Second, they try to convey
positive messages and images

00:07:00.120 --> 00:07:02.650
often focused on the relationship

00:07:02.650 --> 00:07:05.110
with the host country and its leader.

00:07:05.110 --> 00:07:07.480
By sending these messages,

00:07:07.480 --> 00:07:10.630
they attempt to increase the likelihood

00:07:10.630 --> 00:07:13.330
that audience will be a view them

00:07:13.330 --> 00:07:16.053
and by extension their country favorably.

00:07:17.370 --> 00:07:22.230
But both pathways are
contingent on two things

00:07:24.177 --> 00:07:28.333
First, how the opportunity, just a minute.

00:07:29.693 --> 00:07:33.730
How the opportunity for
public diplomacy arranged.

00:07:33.730 --> 00:07:37.710
Usually host leaders have
their own domestic incentives

00:07:37.710 --> 00:07:39.700
to produce publicly visible

00:07:39.700 --> 00:07:42.100
and a positive visit.

00:07:42.100 --> 00:07:44.820
Produce publicly and a positive visit

00:07:44.820 --> 00:07:48.760
which may show up their
own domestic support.

00:07:48.760 --> 00:07:52.160
As invited guests of the
administration in power,

00:07:52.160 --> 00:07:54.440
the visiting leaders typically enjoy

00:07:54.440 --> 00:07:56.880
some level of endorsement
from their hosts,

00:07:56.880 --> 00:07:58.930
increasing their legitimacy

00:07:58.930 --> 00:08:01.550
in the eyes of the foreign public.

00:08:01.550 --> 00:08:04.720
Therefore in most cases,
the host leaders' ability

00:08:04.720 --> 00:08:06.890
to direct domestic attention

00:08:06.890 --> 00:08:09.380
assists the visiting leaders,

00:08:09.380 --> 00:08:12.393
leader in their public diplomacy goals.

00:08:14.410 --> 00:08:17.240
Second, the second is how much one

00:08:17.240 --> 00:08:20.670
and in what ways the media
cover for leaders visit.

00:08:20.670 --> 00:08:23.090
Given the mainstream, I
mean nature of the visit

00:08:23.090 --> 00:08:25.950
and incentives of both leaders,

00:08:25.950 --> 00:08:28.900
the media coverage of high-level visits

00:08:28.900 --> 00:08:31.710
tends to convey positive messages

00:08:31.710 --> 00:08:34.930
and images about the visiting leader.

00:08:34.930 --> 00:08:37.380
So if these conditions are not met

00:08:37.380 --> 00:08:41.250
then a visit will not occur,
but to pretty differently

00:08:41.250 --> 00:08:42.860
of a theory predicts

00:08:42.860 --> 00:08:46.530
that when public diplomacy
visit actually occur,

00:08:46.530 --> 00:08:49.350
it is likely that
incentives of the visiting

00:08:49.350 --> 00:08:52.410
and host leaders are
reasonably well aligned

00:08:52.410 --> 00:08:55.150
and the news media are likely
to produce some coverage

00:08:55.150 --> 00:08:56.210
of the visit.

00:08:56.210 --> 00:09:00.230
Therefore, a visit raises
awareness of the visiting leader

00:09:00.230 --> 00:09:02.120
among citizens of the host country

00:09:02.120 --> 00:09:06.663
and convey positive messages
about that leader to them.

00:09:08.830 --> 00:09:12.030
So given this theory they
can expect the expectation

00:09:12.030 --> 00:09:14.723
of a specific hypothesis
are the following.

00:09:15.970 --> 00:09:19.190
High-level visits increase
favorable perception

00:09:19.190 --> 00:09:21.823
of the visiting leader
in the host country.

00:09:22.880 --> 00:09:25.260
They decrease indifferent perceptions

00:09:26.120 --> 00:09:31.120
and they decrease unfavorable perceptions

00:09:32.360 --> 00:09:36.690
but increasing by increasing awareness

00:09:36.690 --> 00:09:40.180
we think that visit could
increase positive attitudes

00:09:40.180 --> 00:09:42.870
by moving respondents from indifference

00:09:42.870 --> 00:09:45.050
toward a positive opinion.

00:09:45.050 --> 00:09:47.730
By conveying positive messages,

00:09:47.730 --> 00:09:51.623
we also think that visit could
decrease negative attitudes.

00:09:54.359 --> 00:09:59.359
Okay, to test this hypothesis,

00:09:59.660 --> 00:10:04.420
we leverage natural
experiment to our situation

00:10:04.420 --> 00:10:08.770
and estimate causal
effects of high-level visit

00:10:08.770 --> 00:10:11.120
using individual level opinion posts

00:10:11.120 --> 00:10:13.520
generated in the real world environment

00:10:13.520 --> 00:10:16.360
at the time of high-level visits.

00:10:16.360 --> 00:10:20.030
Specifically, we use, sorry.

00:10:20.030 --> 00:10:23.200
We use one of the most
extensive cross national

00:10:23.200 --> 00:10:27.550
and individual level data
available, Gallup World Polls.

00:10:27.550 --> 00:10:29.570
And specifically to the
question when you use

00:10:29.570 --> 00:10:32.320
for the analysis is, do you approve

00:10:32.320 --> 00:10:35.570
or disapprove of the job
performance of the leadership

00:10:35.570 --> 00:10:37.453
of the name of our country.

00:10:39.230 --> 00:10:40.780
We combine this dataset

00:10:40.780 --> 00:10:44.840
with our own original
data concerning high-level

00:10:44.840 --> 00:10:46.283
by political leaders.

00:10:47.760 --> 00:10:48.820
Identification strategies

00:10:48.820 --> 00:10:51.670
for calls or inference is
to compare the respondents

00:10:51.670 --> 00:10:55.260
to this question between
those who are interviewed

00:10:55.260 --> 00:10:59.430
just before the first
day of a high-level visit

00:10:59.430 --> 00:11:02.090
and those who are interviewed just after.

00:11:02.090 --> 00:11:06.380
If there is no other systematic difference

00:11:06.380 --> 00:11:08.940
between respondents
between these come through

00:11:08.940 --> 00:11:11.790
and treatment groups, then we
can estimate the causal effect

00:11:11.790 --> 00:11:14.113
by just simply taking
the difference of means.

00:11:16.230 --> 00:11:19.310
So the combination of the two datasets

00:11:19.310 --> 00:11:21.440
provides an opportunity to identify

00:11:21.440 --> 00:11:26.440
the causal affects of visit by 15 leaders

00:11:26.480 --> 00:11:31.480
from nine countries, over
11 years to 38 countries.

00:11:33.430 --> 00:11:35.500
And we compared the approval

00:11:35.500 --> 00:11:37.330
or disapproval of the visiting leader

00:11:37.330 --> 00:11:42.330
among 32,456 respondents
interviewed either just before

00:11:43.250 --> 00:11:45.843
or just after the first day of that visit.

00:11:47.510 --> 00:11:50.343
So this table shows the
number of cases in our sample.

00:11:51.210 --> 00:11:55.590
And before showing the of
a statistical analysis,

00:11:55.590 --> 00:11:58.160
I like to focus on one specific case

00:11:58.160 --> 00:12:01.240
to illustrate our research design

00:12:01.240 --> 00:12:03.133
and some interesting findings.

00:12:05.560 --> 00:12:07.360
So a case for this analysis

00:12:07.360 --> 00:12:11.050
is Japanese prime minister
visits to South Korea

00:12:11.050 --> 00:12:15.923
from November 1st to November 2nd in 2015.

00:12:18.610 --> 00:12:23.610
So prime minister arrived in
Seoul on November 1st, 2015.

00:12:23.680 --> 00:12:27.120
And within the five days before this date

00:12:28.090 --> 00:12:32.260
81 Korean adults happened to
respond to their political

00:12:32.260 --> 00:12:34.720
instead of respond to the Gallup poll

00:12:34.720 --> 00:12:37.460
after the event, after the visit.

00:12:37.460 --> 00:12:40.340
And among them 72.9% disapprove

00:12:40.340 --> 00:12:45.340
of the job performance of it
and only 17.6% approved of it.

00:12:49.010 --> 00:12:52.190
But within the five
days after his arrival,

00:12:52.190 --> 00:12:56.550
101 Korean citizens
participated in this survey.

00:12:56.550 --> 00:12:57.980
The importance is the percentage

00:12:57.980 --> 00:13:01.820
of this approval dropped to 63.4%.

00:13:01.820 --> 00:13:04.430
The difference is 9% apart.

00:13:04.430 --> 00:13:07.530
On the other hand, the
percentage of approval

00:13:07.530 --> 00:13:10.010
increased to 20.8%.

00:13:10.010 --> 00:13:11.680
The percentage in difference opinions

00:13:11.680 --> 00:13:15.820
also increased from 9.4 to 15.8.

00:13:15.820 --> 00:13:18.690
So these changes are
consistent with our hypothesis.

00:13:18.690 --> 00:13:20.570
And for this particular case

00:13:20.570 --> 00:13:22.340
they changed the statistically significant

00:13:22.340 --> 00:13:24.883
even for these 182 respondents.

00:13:26.400 --> 00:13:29.160
What could explain these
changes in attitudes

00:13:29.160 --> 00:13:30.730
among Korean people?

00:13:30.730 --> 00:13:33.240
There are some factors
that could be consistent

00:13:33.240 --> 00:13:34.323
with our findings.

00:13:35.890 --> 00:13:37.610
The meeting between the leaders of Japan

00:13:37.610 --> 00:13:40.040
and South Korea in 2015

00:13:40.040 --> 00:13:42.910
was the first meeting in the three years.

00:13:42.910 --> 00:13:45.500
There are many contentious
issues, particularly the issue

00:13:45.500 --> 00:13:47.763
of about the so-called
comfort woman issue.

00:13:48.640 --> 00:13:51.500
But the leader of the
two East Asian countries

00:13:51.500 --> 00:13:53.560
agreed to improve their relationship

00:13:53.560 --> 00:13:57.460
by continuing the dialogues
between two countries

00:13:58.370 --> 00:14:00.890
and the prime minister of it (indistinct)

00:14:00.890 --> 00:14:03.940
also smiled and shook their hands.

00:14:03.940 --> 00:14:06.093
And these images were used in the media,

00:14:07.130 --> 00:14:08.680
but there are also some other factors

00:14:08.680 --> 00:14:11.450
that could be inconsistent
with our findings.

00:14:11.450 --> 00:14:13.550
During the two day visits,

00:14:13.550 --> 00:14:18.203
they only had one meeting and
no joint statement was made.

00:14:19.080 --> 00:14:21.950
There was no also no press conference.

00:14:21.950 --> 00:14:24.510
And typically visiting
leaders participate events

00:14:24.510 --> 00:14:25.860
to meet the general public

00:14:25.860 --> 00:14:28.670
or events to display their
friendly relationship.

00:14:28.670 --> 00:14:31.563
But these events are not organized at all.

00:14:34.060 --> 00:14:37.690
But there was an unexpected
event after the meeting.

00:14:37.690 --> 00:14:40.150
So one of the debates during
the previous arrangement

00:14:40.150 --> 00:14:41.900
between the two governments

00:14:41.900 --> 00:14:45.440
was to whether to have lunch together

00:14:45.440 --> 00:14:48.590
after the morning meeting on November 2nd.

00:14:48.590 --> 00:14:52.260
So the Korean government tried
to set this lunch conditional

00:14:52.260 --> 00:14:56.090
on the Japanese government's
pre visit agreement

00:14:56.090 --> 00:14:58.270
to make a joint statement

00:14:58.270 --> 00:15:03.060
which would set the target
date to find solutions

00:15:03.060 --> 00:15:04.593
for the comfort woman issue.

00:15:05.670 --> 00:15:08.920
But because others said
no to such a compromise,

00:15:08.920 --> 00:15:10.490
president Park missed the chance

00:15:10.490 --> 00:15:14.047
to set a friendly lunch
for public diplomacy.

00:15:14.047 --> 00:15:18.460
And after the meeting
ended around 11:30 a.m.,

00:15:18.460 --> 00:15:20.850
President Park asks a
prime minister of it.

00:15:20.850 --> 00:15:25.540
So what is your plan after
this before your departure?

00:15:25.540 --> 00:15:29.280
And other said, we'll
have a Korean barbecue.

00:15:29.280 --> 00:15:31.210
And a guy went to a restaurant

00:15:31.210 --> 00:15:35.050
not necessarily the very
special restaurant in Seoul

00:15:35.050 --> 00:15:39.140
with people from the Japanese
embassy and other delegates.

00:15:39.140 --> 00:15:41.500
And we actually also
know how much they paid

00:15:41.500 --> 00:15:43.480
and they actually told
her how much they paid

00:15:43.480 --> 00:15:46.650
was about $100 per person.

00:15:46.650 --> 00:15:48.110
So it's not that expensive.

00:15:48.110 --> 00:15:50.430
It's expensive for us

00:15:50.430 --> 00:15:53.760
but maybe not so expensive
for the Japanese government.

00:15:53.760 --> 00:15:55.520
And importantly this is the point,

00:15:55.520 --> 00:15:57.390
the barbecue dance was widely covered

